R&C patch 7052: demand-first order of the asynchronous PSO build queue (load stalls, the RE notes s.30).
KZ3 2048's build queue is FIFO. With the 7050 lookahead a miss queues up to ~100 speculative builds, and the build the
walker then waits on runs after all of them: ToD ahead-on2-cold (exe 00420049) waited 36 times for 26.0 s, max 5.1 s,
against 181 waits, max 0.81 s, with LD_PSO_AHEAD=0; the 3 workers ran at ~1000 ms/s each through the level load.
- libs/video/rsx_pso_prio.h (pure C): a priority queue. A build the walker waits on (a demand: a miss outside the
  lookahead, or ld_apso_await on a build that is still queued) goes to the front, so it waits only for the builds
  already running. Lookahead builds are ordered by FIFO distance (the END sequence number of their draw), nearest first,
  also across scans; equal priorities keep submission order.
- Shared compiles (RSX pass 50) stay deadlock-free: the owner of a pending compile is the job queued first. When a job
  is queued or promoted ahead of the queued owner of a compile it needs, the ownership moves to it (rsx_apq_claim;
  rsx_apso_blob gains owner/owner_flag, cleared when a worker takes the owner).
- Worker count: KZ3 2048 fixed 3. Default now logical processors - 8 (the PPU main thread, 4 SPURS SPU threads, a raw
  SPU, the RSX walker, d3d12core's queue thread: the game's busy threads in a ToD load), clamped to [2, 8]: 8 on the
  20-thread i7-12700KF. LD_PSO_ASYNC_THREADS still overrides.
- LD_PSO_AHEAD_BACKLOG=<n>: the lookahead stops scanning while n builds wait (default 0 = no cap); [pso-ahead] counts
  those stops (backlog=), [pso-async] adds promoted= and claims=. LD_PSO_PRIO=0 = the old FIFO (for the A/B).
Host test port/rc/tests/run_pso_prio_test.sh: order; a simulated load (3 running, 100 queued) where the demanded build
starts when the first running one ends (FIFO negative control: 10.2 s later); a shared compile taken over by the demand
(no-claim negative control deadlocks one worker); a fuzz of 60,000 steps checking that no queued job is ahead of its
compile's owner (fails at step 25 without the claim); the worker count.

